[{:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://simm.is/blog/write-the-model-once/", :title "Probabilistic programming for Clojure programmers: write the model once, choose the inference later", :link "https://simm.is/blog/write-the-model-once/", :published-at #inst "2026-10-16T00:00:00.000-00:00", :content "If you have used core.logic, you already know half of probabilistic programming. A logic program states constraints and lets the system search for the values that satisfy them. A probabilistic program does the same, except that a constraint can be soft: a value that fits the data poorly is not ruled out, only made less likely. Two dice show the idea below. A small forecasting model then runs under several inference methods in Foerster, an MIT-licensed probabilistic programming library for Clojure, and every result is checked against an exact answer. Foerster continues the Anglican line of probabilistic programming in Clojure. Christian Weilbach, who wrote it, did his PhD in Frank Wood’s group at the University of British Columbia, where Anglican was built. What is a probabilistic program? Take two dice and the constraint that they sum to 7. Without any library, the plainest way to find the pairs that meet it is to generate pairs at random and keep the ones that pass: Superficie Clojure What is this syntax? defn roll []:\n  inc(rand-int(6))\nend\n\ndefn generate-and-test [n]:\n  loop [kept []]:\n    if n = count(kept):\n      kept\n    else:\n      a := roll()\n      b := roll()\n      recur(if 7 = a + b:\n              conj(kept, [a b])\n            else:\n              kept\n            end)\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nfrequencies(generate-and-test(6000))\n;; each of [1 6] [2 5] [3 4] [4 3] [5 2] [6 1] about 1000 times (defn roll [] (inc (rand-int 6)))\n\n(defn generate-and-test [n]\n  (loop [kept []]\n    (if (= n (count kept))\n      kept\n      (let [a (roll) b (roll)]\n        (recur (if (= 7 (+ a b)) (conj kept [a b]) kept))))))\n\n(frequencies (generate-and-test 6000))\n;; each of [1 6] [2 5] [3 4] [4 3] [5 2] [6 1] about 1000 times The random choices form a generative model, a small simulator of two dice. The test conditions the simulator on what we know. What survives is the posterior: the pairs that are still possible, and how often each occurs. A probabilistic program states the same model without fixing how to search it: Superficie Clojure What is this syntax? defn dice []:\n  sp/spin:\n    a := sample(dist/uniform-discrete(1, 7), :id, :a)\n    b := sample(dist/uniform-discrete(1, 7), :id, :b)\n    factor(if 7 = a + b:\n      0.0\n    else:\n      ##-Inf\n    end)\n    [a b]\n  end\nend (defn dice []\n  (sp/spin\n   (let [a (sample (dist/uniform-discrete 1 7) :id :a)\n         b (sample (dist/uniform-discrete 1 7) :id :b)]\n     (factor (if (= 7 (+ a b)) 0.0 ##-Inf))\n     [a b]))) sample introduces an unknown, as a fresh logic variable does in core.logic, but with a probability on each value: here a die whose six faces are equally likely. factor adds to the run’s log weight. Zero leaves the run unchanged, and minus infinity makes it impossible, as a failing goal does. sp/spin turns the body into a program that an inference method can pause, copy and resume at each sample and factor. With {:method :enumerate}, Foerster runs all 36 pairs and returns the six that hold, each with probability 1/6. That is the answer run* gives, with weights. Now soften the constraint. We do not know the sum, but a sensor that measures it with noise of about one showed 7: Superficie Clojure What is this syntax? observe(dist/normal(a + b, 1.0), 7.0, :id, :reading) (observe (dist/normal (+ a b) 1.0) 7.0 :id :reading) observe weights each run by how likely the reading is under it, here a normal density centred on the run’s sum. No pair is ruled out, and the arithmetic can be done by hand. Each of the six pairs that sum to 7 gets weight e⁰ = 1, each of the five pairs that sum to 6 or to 8 gets e^−½ ≈ 0.61, and the weights keep falling with the distance from 7. Normalized, a sum of 7 has probability 0.45, sums of 6 and 8 have 0.23 each, sums of 5 and 9 have 0.04 each, and the rest almost nothing. Foerster’s enumeration gives the same numbers. core.logic Foerster a fresh logic variable sample, an unknown with a probability on each value a goal such as ==, which holds or fails observe or factor, a weight; failing is weight zero conde, a choice between branches a discrete sample, with a probability on each branch run*, the set of answers the posterior, the answers with their weights the search strategy the inference method Two more ideas carry over. The total weight before normalizing, called the evidence, says how well the model as a whole explains the data. A hard constraint that no run meets leaves nothing, and a soft one leaves a number that can be compared between models. And as core.logic leaves the search to the system, a probabilistic program leaves the inference method open. ProbLog joins the two traditions as probabilistic logic programming. How does the system find the answer? Enumeration works for 36 pairs. It fails for many choices, or for continuous ones, because no list of all runs exists. The other methods search the space of runs, and each resembles a familiar strategy: Importance sampling runs the program many times and weights each run instead of discarding it. It is generate and test with soft scores, and it fails when good runs are rare. Sequential Monte Carlo (SMC) runs many copies side by side. At each observation it copies the runs that fit and drops the ones that do not, like a search that prunes partial solutions as soon as a constraint can be checked. Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) changes one choice of a single run at a time and keeps the change by a rule that sometimes accepts a worse run. This local search visits runs in proportion to their weight. Does the choice of method matter? A shop sells around a hundred units of a product each week. The weekly figure is noisy, and the underlying demand drifts. How high is demand now, and how certain is that? The model says that demand starts near 100, moves by a random amount each week, and that each week’s figure is demand plus noise: Superficie Clojure What is this syntax? defn demand [weeks drift]:\n  sp/spin:\n    loop [t 0, level sample(dist/normal(100.0, 20.0), :id, [:level 0])]:\n      observe(dist/normal(level, 8.0), nth(weeks, t), :id, [:y t])\n      if inc(t) = count(weeks):\n        level\n      else:\n        recur(inc(t), sample(dist/normal(level, drift), :id, [:level inc(t)]))\n      end\n    end\n  end\nend (defn demand [weeks drift]\n  (sp/spin\n   (loop [t 0\n          level (sample (dist/normal 100.0 20.0) :id [:level 0])]\n     (observe (dist/normal level 8.0) (nth weeks t) :id [:y t])\n     (if (= (inc t) (count weeks))\n       level\n       (recur (inc t)\n              (sample (dist/normal level drift) :id [:level (inc t)])))))) As with the dice, sample marks the unknowns, here twenty-one weekly demand levels, and observe scores each run against the twenty sales figures. The data are an ordinary argument, weeks. The :id names each choice, so a method can refer to the level in week 7 across runs. The pictures article explains priors and posteriors if those terms are new. Each method is one option of the same call: Superficie Clojure What is this syntax? infer/infer(demand(weeks, 4.0), {:method :smc, :particles 1000})\ninfer/infer(demand(weeks, 4.0), {:method :importance, :particles 1000})\ninfer/infer(demand(weeks, 4.0),\n  {:method :mh, :iterations 4000, :chains 4, :burn 1000})\ninfer/infer(demand(weeks, 4.0),\n  {:method :pgibbs, :particles 50, :iterations 600}) (infer/infer (demand weeks 4.0) {:method :smc :particles 1000})\n(infer/infer (demand weeks 4.0) {:method :importance :particles 1000})\n(infer/infer (demand weeks 4.0) {:method :mh :iterations 4000 :chains 4 :burn 1000})\n(infer/infer (demand weeks 4.0) {:method :pgibbs :particles 50 :iterations 600}) The model is linear and Gaussian, so a Kalman filter computes the exact answer, which makes it a fair test. The data are twenty simulated weeks, with a true drift of 4 per week and observation noise of 8. Each particle method ran ten times with different seeds, so the table shows how much an answer varies from run to run. All numbers come from Foerster’s main branch on 15 October 2026. Method Final level (mean) Log evidence Check Exact (Kalman filter) 102.89 −77.49 Sequential Monte Carlo, 1000 particles 102.92 ± 0.23 −77.44 ± 0.14 about 120 histories survive to week 0 Importance sampling, 1000 samples 102.9 ± 1.8 −77.7 ± 0.5 2 to 10 effective samples Metropolis–Hastings, 4 chains 103.4 R-hat 1.03, 114 effective draws Particle Gibbs, 4 chains 102.93 R-hat 1.002, about 1500 effective draws SMC lands within 0.36 of the exact final level in every run, and its log evidence averages −77.44 against the exact −77.49. It pays for its pruning on the early weeks: of a thousand particles at the end, only 114 to 134 still descend from different starting points. Importance sampling never prunes, so after twenty weeks a handful of runs carry nearly all the weight, and the estimate moves by almost two units between runs. Metropolis–Hastings (MH), the simplest MCMC, fails here, and its diagnostics say so before any exact answer is consulted. Its four chains disagree: their rank-normalized R-hat is 1.03, above the usual threshold of 1.01, and their 12,000 draws are worth about 114 independent ones. Foerster computes these numbers as Stan and ArviZ do. The cause lies in the model. Neighbouring weeks are strongly tied, so changing one week’s level at a time barely moves the run. Andrew Gelman’s folk theorem of statistical computing, restated in the Bayesian workflow paper, says as much: when the computation struggles, look at the model. The fix is a method that changes all weeks together. Particle Gibbs runs a whole SMC through the twenty weeks at every step, holding one surviving run fixed, and so redraws all twenty-one levels at once. Four chains of 500 steps after warm-up, with 50 particles each, reached an R-hat of 1.002 and about 1500 effective draws of the final level, counting each step’s weighted average as one draw. Their mean was 102.93 against the exact 102.89. The first week’s level, which SMC sees through only a few surviving histories, reached an R-hat of 1.002 and about 660 effective draws. Particle Gibbs took four minutes against half a minute for MH, but produced about twice as many effective draws per second. Nothing in the model changed, only the search. Twenty weeks of observed demand with the inferred demand level: the exact filtered mean and the 95% band from sequential Monte Carlo. 90 100 110 120 130 140 1 5 10 15 20 week observed SMC level, 95% band exact (Kalman filter) Each point is one week's observed demand. The shaded band is where sequential Monte Carlo puts the hidden demand level with 95% probability after that week, and the dashed line is the exact answer, which this simple model happens to allow. The two lines nearly coincide. What if a parameter is unknown too? So far the drift was given as 4. In practice nobody measures how fast demand moves, so the drift becomes one more unknown with a prior: Superficie Clojure What is this syntax? defn demand-unknown [weeks]:\n  sp/spin:\n    drift := sample(dist/gamma(2.0, 2.5), :id, :drift)\n    loop [t 0, level sample(dist/normal(100.0, 20.0), :id, [:level 0])]:\n      observe(dist/normal(level, 8.0), nth(weeks, t), :id, [:y t])\n      if inc(t) = count(weeks):\n        drift\n      else:\n        recur(inc(t), sample(dist/normal(level, drift), :id, [:level inc(t)]))\n      end\n    end\n  end\nend\n\ninfer/infer(demand-unknown(weeks),\n  {:method :pmmh, :particles 100, :iterations 2000, :params #{:drift},\n   :scale 3.0, :burn 200}) (defn demand-unknown [weeks]\n  (sp/spin\n   (let [drift (sample (dist/gamma 2.0 2.5) :id :drift)]\n     (loop [t 0\n            level (sample (dist/normal 100.0 20.0) :id [:level 0])]\n       (observe (dist/normal level 8.0) (nth weeks t) :id [:y t])\n       (if (= (inc t) (count weeks))\n         drift\n         (recur (inc t)\n                (sample (dist/normal level drift) :id [:level (inc t)])))))))\n\n(infer/infer (demand-unknown weeks)\n             {:method :pmmh :particles 100 :iterations 2000\n              :params #{:drift} :scale 3.0 :burn 200}) Particle marginal Metropolis–Hastings (PMMH) searches over the drift alone. For each proposed drift it runs SMC over the weekly levels and accepts or rejects the drift by the evidence that SMC reports. That evidence estimate is unbiased, so the chain targets the exact posterior of the drift even though every step uses an estimate. The posterior distribution of the weekly drift: a histogram of PMMH draws over the exact density computed by quadrature. 0 2 4 6 8 10 12 weekly drift (sd of the level change) simulated with 4 PMMH draws exact posterior Twenty weeks of data narrow the drift only partly: values from below 1 to about 6 remain plausible. The histogram of PMMH draws follows the exact density, which quadrature over the Kalman filter's evidence provides for this model. The chain’s mean drift is 2.82 against an exact 2.85, and its 90% interval runs from 0.61 to 6.09 against an exact 0.61 to 6.28. The data were simulated with a drift of 4, inside the interval but not at its centre, because twenty noisy weeks cannot pin the drift down. A forecast with one fitted drift would hide that width. The 2000 iterations took five and a half minutes on a laptop. For this linear Gaussian model, Stan or a Kalman filter inside a one-parameter chain would take seconds. PMMH is for models without a gradient to follow, models that branch, count or call a simulator. How do I run it? Foerster is on Clojars. With the Clojure CLI: {:deps {org.replikativ/foerster {:mvn/version \"0.1.113\"}}} A model runs inside a Spindel world, an execution context, and inference returns a spin that the REPL dereferences: Superficie Clojure What is this syntax? require('[org.replikativ.foerster.core :as infer :refer [sample observe factor]],\n  '[org.replikativ.foerster.dist :as dist],\n  '[org.replikativ.spindel.core :as sp])\n\ndef world: sp/create-execution-context()\n\nsp/with-context world:\n  @infer/infer(demand(weeks, 4.0), {:method :smc, :particles 1000})\nend (require '[org.replikativ.foerster.core :as infer :refer [sample observe factor]]\n         '[org.replikativ.foerster.dist :as dist]\n         '[org.replikativ.spindel.core :as sp])\n\n(def world (sp/create-execution-context))\n\n(sp/with-context world\n  @(infer/infer (demand weeks 4.0) {:method :smc :particles 1000})) Build the model inside with-context, as here: outside a world, the program has nowhere to run. The scripts that produced every number above are in the Foerster repository. What can Foerster not do yet? Foerster is beta software. Beyond the methods shown here, it implements tempered and waste-free SMC, SMC², involutive MCMC with reversible jump, parallel tempering, the No-U-Turn sampler on numerical blocks, MAP estimation, the Laplace approximation, Pathfinder, and variational inference. The methods notebook describes each one with its papers, and the gallery works through classic models against exact answers. For a static model with continuous parameters, Stan, PyMC and NumPyro remain the mature choice. Foerster’s No-U-Turn sampler works on numerical blocks whose gradient the model supplies or Raster compiles. On one hierarchical model with 89 parameters, it matched PyMC’s warm run with a gradient compiled by Raster, and ran 1.5 times faster with a hand-written one. Its general methods over program traces are slower: single-site MH takes about twelve times as long per move as Gen, because each move replays the rest of the program, and its particle filter is about four and a half times slower than Gen’s Unfold combinator. The comparison has the details, and coming from other systems maps Stan, PyMC, Turing, Gen, Anglican and WebPPL concepts onto Foerster, including what is missing. What comes next? Every model so far is a pure function. Useful models often are not: a forecast that reads current stock, a simulation that writes into a scenario database, an agent that calls tools. If a thousand particles write to the same database, inference corrupts the state it reasons about, which is why Gen requires that a model change no external state. Foerster runs each particle in a Spindel world, whose state can be copied cheaply, as the copy-on-write article describes. When SMC copies a particle, it copies the particle’s world, and when it drops a particle, it discards the world. With :world-policy :fork, every particle starts from a copy of the caller’s world, so a model can read and change the systems registered there, and nothing a particle writes reaches the caller. This costs about three times as much as running in fresh worlds. Systems that cannot be copied safely, such as live network handles, are refused before the model runs, and Markov chains run in fresh worlds only. The same mechanism makes counterfactuals direct: intervene on one choice in the world where the posterior was computed (Pearl’s do operator, as in the causal graph article) and replay the program with everything else held to what was inferred. The worlds guide covers both, and a later article shows them on a model that books orders into a forked database. The same machinery steers processes that are not statistical models. Foerster’s steer namespace runs SMC over the steps of an agent working on a task, following twisted SMC (Zhao et al., 2024) and the SMC steering of language models (Lew et al., 2023). On short spreadsheet tasks it matched picking the best of four attempts with 17% fewer tokens, but solved nothing more. It should pay on long tasks whose steps can be checked cheaply. For Simmis, inference over forked worlds is research rather than a product feature. It is how a governed organizational record could later answer a question such as “what happens to our cash if this order slips” with a distribution instead of a single number."}, :excerpts [{:text "Probabilistic programming for Clojure programmers: write the model once, choose the...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :title} {:text "... probabilistic programming library for Clojure, and every result is checked against an exact...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... Anglican line of probabilistic programming in Clojure. Christian Weilbach, who wrote it, did his PhD in...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :title "vim-slime", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :published-at #inst "2026-10-16T03:41:33.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> vim-slime </h1> <p> Mar 26, 2012 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>Today I found the awesomeness that is <a href=\"https://github.com/jpalardy/vim-slime\">vim-slime</a>, it's been an exciting day for me. <a href=\"http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/\">Slime</a> is the \"The Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for Emacs\", I can almost hear the emacs crowd laughing.</p> <p>For those that use vim and haven't used Slime, vim-slime or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's awesome:</p> <p><strong>Text can be sent from any process to the stdin of a <a href=\"http://www.gnu.org/software/screen/\">gnu screen</a> or <a href=\"http://tmux.sourceforge.net/\">tmux</a> session. The process in this case is vim and the screen/tmux session is a terminal</strong>.</p> <p>Screen is a <a href=\"/journal/oh-screen-where-have-you-been\">really neat</a> terminal multiplexer (you can run multiple terminals in a terminal window). The multiplexed shell processes are children of the screen process, which itself is not a child of the terminal window process. This means a screen process and its child processes keep running if you close the terminal window. Later you can re-connect to it, this is what makes vim-slime possible.</p> <p>Here is an screen shot, on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim slime screenshot\" src=\"screenshot.jpg\"/></p> <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs that use STDIN.</p> <p>This concept can be taken even further, You can even connect to a tmux session over SSH and share a terminal or a <a href=\"http://remotepairprogramming.com/remote-pair-programming-with-tmux-and-vim-the\">terminal program like vim to do remote pairing</a>!</p> <p>Hopefully remote pairing is the topic of my next post as there are a couple geographically distant people I know who are keen to do some pair hacking. I stand to learn a lot!</p> <p><a name=\"footnote-1\">[1]</a> I learnt almost everything I know about Lisp from <a href=\"http://www.ccs.neu.edu/home/matthias/BTLS/\">The Little Schemer</a>. Great book.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl"], :source :content} {:text "... <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl" "command"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :title "Swipe Keyboard", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :published-at #inst "2026-10-16T03:41:33.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <style> #swipe-canvas { position: relative; width: 900px; height: 300px; } #swipe-results { font-size: 30px; padding-left: 50px; padding-left: 50px; } #swipe-results ul { margin: 0px; padding: 0px; } #swipe-results li { float: left; background-color: #DDDDDD; list-style-type: none; padding: 10px; margin: 5px; border-radius: 5px; } #swipe { position: relative; } #swipe-loading { position: absolute; height: 50px; width: 300px; top: 85px; left: 300px; background-color: darkgray; border-radius: 10px; text-align: center; padding-top: 20px; border: black; border-width: 5px; } </style> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> Swipe Keyboard </h1> <p> Apr 06, 2014 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>When I first tried a <a href=\"http://www.swype.com/\">Swype</a> keyboard I was impressed how effective it was. Even though I don't use the feature on my phone I was interested in how it could be built, so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard\">implemented this otherwise useless swipe-able keyboard</a> below. It probably doesn't work on mobile devices, but works on modern browsers with mouse pointers (although I've only really tried Chrome and Firefox).</p> <div id=\"swipe\"> <canvas height=\"300px\" id=\"swipe-canvas\" width=\"900px\"></canvas> <div id=\"swipe-results\"></div> <div style=\"clear: both\"></div> <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially tried to solve this using the technique Peter Norvig famously uses in his <a href=\"http://norvig.com/spell-correct.html]\">spell checker</a>. He takes a sequence of characters and generates a set of word candidates by adding, removing and swapping characters in the original sequence, the generated candidates are removed if they are not found a dictionary. This can work but to be effective too many combinations need to be generated.</p> <p>If the dictionary is indexed into a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trie\">trie</a> the number of combinations generated can be reduced significantly by traversing the trie and only generating valid letter combinations. This is a pretty bare implementation of that, it requires: </p> <ul> <li>The first and last characters of the initial sequence are used </li> <li>Intermediate characters in the initial sequence can be repeated or discarded </li> <li>No characters are added or swapped</li> </ul> <p>Basically, if you swipe through all the characters in a word in order, then the word will be found if it is in the index regardless how many characters are swiped in between. It is surprisingly quick and effective.</p> <p>This implementation uses <a href=\"https://raw.github.com/first20hours/google-10000-english/master/google-10000-english.txt\">these 10000 words</a>, I intended to use digital books but never got around to it as these words demonstrate the concept well enough.</p> <p>This is the first thing I've written in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from non-idiomatic to shamblolic. I initially used a <a href=\"http://clojuredocs.org/clojure_core/clojure.zip/zipper\">zipper</a> to build the trie with immutable data structures, but found the indexing took to long with my zipper implementation so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard/commit/6edd7b26e78121fbe8586b3f0ef54ca8277d9e32\">switched to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/core.async\">core.async</a> library is really awesome, the <a href=\"http://docs.closure-library.googlecode.com/git/index.html\">Google closure library</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/closure/compiler/\">compiler</a> integration with <a href=\"http://leiningen.org/\">Leiningen</a> the <a href=\"https://github.com/emezeske/lein-cljsbuild\">cljsbuild plug-in</a> to be impressive. My main pains were the slow JVM start-up time, the advanced closure compiler build of the web worker script fails silently when run (but the main script works fine when compiled with the advanced compiler), and at times I felt some compile time type checking would be nice.</p> <p>I would like to extend this experiment to index the word occurrence counts and proceeding word counts in original text and rank the found words as most likely. Support casing, umlauts, special characters, spelling correction and compound words in the indexing and lookup. I think a live lookup while swiping would also be possible.</p> <p>Overall this was fun, turned out OK I think, and was a great learning experience.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> <script src=\"swipe.js\" type=\"text/javascript\"></script> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content} {:text "... in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from...", :matched-terms ["rescript"], :source :content} {:text "... to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content}]}]